First, we calculate \(T(b+1) - T(b)\). By the definition of the triangular number \(T(n) = \frac{n(n+1)}{2}\), we have: \[
\begin{align*}
T(b+1) &= \frac{(b+1)(b+2)}{2}, \\
\end{align*}
\] \[
\begin{align*}
T(b) &= \frac{b(b+1)}{2}.
\end{align*}
\] Therefore, \[
\begin{align*}
T(b+1) - T(b) &= \frac{(b+1)(b+2)}{2} - \frac{b(b+1)}{2} \\
&= \frac{(b+1)(b+2) - b(b+1)}{2} \\
&= \frac{(b+1)(b+2 - b)}{2} \\
&= \frac{(b+1) \cdot 2}{2} \\
&= b+1.
\end{align*}
\] Since \(T(b+1) - T(b) = T(x)\) and \(T(x) = \frac{x(x+1)}{2}\), we need \(b+1 = \frac{x(x+1)}{2}\). This simplifies to: \[
\begin{align*}
x(x+1) &= 2(b+1).
\end{align*}
\] We need to find the smallest integer \(b > 2011\) such that \(2(b+1)\) can be written as \(x(x+1)\) for some integer \(x\). Since \(b > 2011\), we have \(b+1 > 2012\). We need to find the smallest integer \(x\) such that \(x(x+1) > 4024\). We can find \(x\) by taking the positive integer part of the square root of 4024. \[
\begin{align*}
\sqrt{4024} \approx 63.434.
\end{align*}
\] So, the smallest possible \(x\) is 64. We can now check if \(64 \cdot 65 = 4160\) which is greater than 4024 and satisfies \(64^2 + 64 = 4096\), and check for the next values. We continue this process until we find the correct \(x\). \[
\begin{align*}
65 \cdot 66 = 4290, \quad 64 \cdot 65 = 4160, \quad 63 \cdot 64 = 4032, \quad 62 \cdot 63 = 3894.
\end{align*}
\] So, the smallest \(b\) such that \(2(b+1)\) can be written as \(x(x+1)\) is when \(x = 64\), giving us \(2(b+1) = 64 \cdot 65 = 4160\). Solving for \(b\), we get: \[
\begin{align*}
b+1 &= 2080, \\
b &= 2079.
\end{align*}
\] However, \(b\) must be greater than 2011, so we need to check the next possible \(b\). For \(x = 65\): \[
\begin{align*}
x(x+1) = 65 \cdot 66 = 4290, \\
b+1 = 2145, \\
b = 2144.
\end{align*}
\] Therefore, the smallest integer \(b > 2011\) such that \(T(b+1) - T(b) = T(x)\) is \(b = 2144\). </think> <answer>2144</answer>

